{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "מכל חטאת האדם", :translit "mikol chatat ha-adam", :gloss "from every sin of the person"} {:he "שילוח הטמאים", :translit "shiluach ha-temei'im", :gloss "sending out of the impure"} {:he "חטאת האדם", :translit "chatat ha-adam", :gloss "sin of the person"} {:he "מצורע", :translit "metzora", :gloss "a person afflicted with tzara'at"} {:he "זב", :translit "zav", :gloss "a man with an abnormal genital discharge"} {:he "טמא לנפש", :translit "tamei la-nefesh", :gloss "impure through contact with a corpse"} {:he "מות תמות", :translit "mot tamut", :gloss "you shall surely die"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of parashah sections: the confession for sins immediately follows the laws of sending out the impure (Numbers 5:2–4)."} {:kind :other, :note "Reading the three categories of impurity in Numbers 5:2–3 (metzora, zav, tamei la-nefesh) as corresponding to the three parties punished in Genesis 3: serpent, woman, man."}], :frame {:text "The serpent was cursed above all beasts (Genesis 3:14). The woman was cursed with increased pain in childbirth (Genesis 3:16). The man was cursed to return to dust — 'for dust you are and to dust you shall return' (Genesis 3:19). The phrase 'mot tamut' appears in Genesis 2:17 as the warning before the sin and again in Genesis 3:19 as the consequence.", :sources ["Genesis 3:14" "Genesis 3:16" "Genesis 3:19" "Genesis 2:17"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ל", :translit "lamed", :gloss "lamed [the letter]"} {:he "מעול מעל", :translit "ma'ol ma'al", :gloss "acting treacherously — acting treacherously"} {:he "בה'", :translit "bah", :gloss "in it [with the letter heh]"} {:he "מטה דינו של גר", :translit "matah dino shel ger", :gloss "perverts the judgment of a convert"} {:he "מטה דינו של מעלה", :translit "matah dino shel ma'alah", :gloss "perverts the judgment of Above"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Extraneous letter lamed added to the form מעול → מעלים to produce the doubled expression מעול מעל."} {:kind :other, :note "The added heh in בה is read as a marker indicating a second level of meaning — that perverting earthly justice is tantamount to perverting divine justice."}], :frame {:text "Sanhedrin 7b states that any judge who perverts judgment is called an enemy of God, as it is written 'He who says to the wicked, You are righteous — the nations will curse him' (Proverbs 24:24). Shabbat 10a states that every judge who judges with complete truth is considered a partner of the Holy One in Creation.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 7b" "Shabbat 10a" "Proverbs 24:24"]}} {:quotes [{:he "הנפש ההוא והתודו", :translit "ha-nefesh ha-hu ve-hitvadu", :gloss "that person — and they shall confess"} {:he "המומתים מתודים", :translit "ha-mumtim mitvadim", :gloss "those who are executed make confession"} {:he "בעת יציאת הנפש", :translit "be-et yetzi'at ha-nefesh", :gloss "at the time of the soul's departure"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The phrase 'ha-nefesh ha-hu' (Numbers 5:6) is juxtaposed to 've-hitvadu' (Numbers 5:7) to hint that at the moment the soul departs, confession is made."}], :frame {:text "Sanhedrin 43a records that those condemned to execution were given a strengthening drink of frankincense in wine so that they would not lose clarity, and they were encouraged to confess, for whoever confesses gains a share in the World to Come. The Mishnah Sanhedrin 6:2 describes how the court would say to the condemned, 'Confess, for whoever confesses gains a share in the World to Come.'", :sources ["Sanhedrin 43a" "Mishnah Sanhedrin 6:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of parashah sections: the confession for sins immediately follows the laws of sending out the impure (Numbers 5:2–4).", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Reading the three categories of impurity in Numbers 5:2–3 (metzora, zav, tamei la-nefesh) as corresponding to the three parties punished in Genesis 3: serpent, woman, man.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Extraneous letter lamed added to the form מעול → מעלים to produce the doubled expression מעול מעל.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "The added heh in בה is read as a marker indicating a second level of meaning — that perverting earthly justice is tantamount to perverting divine justice.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "The phrase 'ha-nefesh ha-hu' (Numbers 5:6) is juxtaposed to 've-hitvadu' (Numbers 5:7) to hint that at the moment the soul departs, confession is made.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:27:07.369369002Z", :model "glm-5.2"}